European farm ministers meet in  Luxembourg on the twenty-fourth of June  hoping to clinch a political agreement  on major reforms of the e use vast  common agricultural policy for can the  negotiations are dragged on for around  18 months as working groups sought to  narrow down a package that will maintain  living standards for farmers and provide  secure food supplies at fair prices to  consumers it's the first time that the  reform has to be approved by all three  EU institutions the council the European  Parliament and the Commission the Irish  presidency is determined to seal a deal  by the time its term expires on jun 30  so it's invited members of parliament to  Luxembourg to discuss the outstanding  issues the cap has proved notoriously  difficult to reform and is regularly the  subject of heated debate spending on  agriculture accounts for a big slice of  the annual EU budget stover thirty  percent this year although it has been  decreasing in size the global economic  downturn has made negotiations tougher  particularly as the 27 member nations  strove to finalize the e usenext  long-term budget but farmers and  consumers alike are counting on a deal  now the reforms are focused on rules on  direct payments to farmers a common  organization of the markets in  agricultural products rural development  support and the way the cap is financed  managed monitored officials aren't  cautiously optimistic that an important  political agreement can be reached at  this council and it's possible that the  meeting will run on a 2-1 is found  should that happen the Accord would have  to be confirmed by Parliament
 

